 file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6015a1f1.gif","alt":"This figure is a line graph that displays the data from 45 reporting areas (excluding Alaska, California, Louisiana, Maryland, New Hampshire, Oklahoma, and West Virginia) for 1999-2008 showing the number of abortions, the abortion rate (i.e., the number of abortions per 1,000 women aged 15-44 years), and the abortion ratio (i.e., the number of abortions per 1,000 live births) per year. The number of reported abortions decreased by 1,004 abortions per year, and the abortion rate actually increased by 0.02 abortions per 1,000 women per year. The change in abortion ratios was more consistent. On average, abortion ratios decreased by 2.7 and 2.5 abortions per 1,000 live births per year, respectively, during 1999-2003 and 2004-2008 Compared with 2006, the total number and rate of reported abortions in 2007 was 2% lower and the abortion ratio was 3% lower. Compared with 1998, the total number, rate, and ratio of reported abortions in 2007 were 6%, 7%, and 14% lower, respectively.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":401,"renderedHeight":346,"naturalWidth":401,"naturalHeight":346,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 988 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":8960},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6015a1f2.gif","alt":"This figure is a bar graph that displays data from 47 reporting areas (excludes California, Florida, Maryland, New Hampshire, and Wyoming) for 2008 showing the abortion rate (i.e., the number of abortions per 1,000 women aged 15-44 years, the abortion ratio (i.e., the number of abortions per 1,000 live births), and the percentage of total abortions by the age group of women who obtained a legal abortion in the United States in 2008. Women aged 20-29 years accounted for the majority (57.1%) of abortions and had the highest abortion rates (29.6 and 21.6 abortions per 1,000 women aged 20-24 and 25-29 years, respectively). Women in the youngest and oldest age groups (aged <15 or =40 years) accounted for the smallest percentage of abortions (0.5% and 3.1%, respectively) and had the lowest abortion rates (1.2 and 2.7 abortions per 1,000 women aged <15 and =40 years, respectively).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":399,"renderedHeight":303,"naturalWidth":399,"naturalHeight":303,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 887 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":11232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6015a1f3.gif","alt":"This figure is a bar graph that displays data for 40 reporting areas (excludes California, Connecticut, District of Columbia, Florida, Illinois, Maryland, Massachusetts, Nebraska, Nevada, New Hampshire, Wisconsin, and Wyoming) showing the percentage of women who obtained abortions at =8 weeks' gestation, 9-13 weeks' gestation, or >13 weeks' gestation in 2008. The largest percentage of abortions was obtained by =8 weeks' gestation. However, whereas 44.0% of adolescents aged <15 years and 53.2% of adolescents aged 15-19 years obtained an abortion by =8 weeks' gestation, 61.2%-70.0% of women aged =20 years obtained an abortion by this point in gestation. Conversely, 19.0% of adolescents aged <15 years and 12.3% of adolescents 15-19 years obtained an abortion after 13 weeks' gestation, whereas this percentage ranged from 6.8%-8.9% for adult women.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":404,"renderedHeight":309,"naturalWidth":404,"naturalHeight":309,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 855 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":10835},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_word.gif","alt":"Microsoft Word file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":1087},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_excel.gif","alt":"Microsoft Excel file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":644},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_ppt.gif","alt":"Microsoft PowerPoint file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"load